CloudBurrow patch 83 to github.com/goccy/bigquery-emulator v0.8.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1655). Needs patches 0002 (cloudburrow_job_order,
keepResult), 0007 (jobKeys) and 0009 and googlesqlite 0019
(QueryRawContext, EncodeString).

The bug: UpdateJob (each query job's result), DeleteJob, jobExists and
keepResult's expiry of an old result each wrote or read one job with a
GoogleSQL WHERE on the jobs table's key, which googlesqlite compiles to a
function call per row, so SQLite answered each with a SCAN of every job the
emulator kept, and DeleteJob a second of cloudburrow_job_order. The
CloudBurrow front deletes each job of its own once it has read it
(forgetInternalJob), so each query a client made cost several such scans,
under the emulator's one request lock (sequentialAccessMiddleware), and
every request waited longer with every job kept.

Worst was keepResult. Once the results kept first pass ResultsMaxBytes,
the next result drops the oldest until they fit again, one UPDATE (one
scan) each; a result of 7 MB (an INFORMATION_SCHEMA.JOBS_BY_ORGANIZATION
of 9,250 jobs) after thousands of small ones dropped them all at once.
Measured on an instance that had run the BigQuery compat suite and 6,000
more jobs (arm64, Docker Desktop), in TestBigQueryInformationSchemaJobs
-count=200: runs 20 and 21 each held the request lock in keepResult for
over 60 s (goroutine dumps, patch 0082), every other request waiting, and
failed on their 60 s deadline; the runs took 5.7 s at 9,020 jobs and 8 s
at 9,680.

Each now runs as SQLite SQL keyed by (projectID, id), as findJobRow reads
one job (patch 0009), in the request's transaction: connection.Conn.Begin
binds each transaction to its connection (metadata.BindTx), and the
repository reaches SQLite through it. A transaction no connection is bound
to is written with GoogleSQL, as before.
cloudburrow_job_by_key_test.go checks each statement's query plan is a
SEARCH of the primary key, and that both paths write and delete the same
rows; results_test.go's inTx binds its transactions, so the tests of
patches 0002 and 0009 run on the new path.

